WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.708
A factory makes one hundred products.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.516
Every day it gets a thousand orders

3
00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:10.186
one per sales region.

4
00:00:10.186 --> 00:00:12.387
The matrix A stays the same

5
00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:14.740
only the right side b changes.

6
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.236
We could eliminate from scratch

7
00:00:18.337 --> 00:00:19.961
for every order

8
00:00:19.961 --> 00:00:23.614
or factor A once and reuse L and U.

9
00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:27.166
How much does factoring once save?

10
00:00:27.166 --> 00:00:28.689
Twice as fast?

11
00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:29.805
Ten times?

12
00:00:29.805 --> 00:00:31.530
Pause and guess.

13
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.279
Count the work.

14
00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:38.849
The inner step of elimination is

15
00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:41.264
one multiply and one subtract.

16
00:00:41.264 --> 00:00:42.900
For the first pivot

17
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.783
there are n minus one rows below it

18
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:48.977
each with n minus one entries to update.

19
00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:52.093
The next pivot has n minus two of each

20
00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:52.950
and so on.

21
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.837
Adding those squares gives about

22
00:00:55.916 --> 00:00:57.898
n cubed over three steps.

23
00:00:57.977 --> 00:01:00.831
The forward and back solves together

24
00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.971
take only about n squared

25
00:01:02.971 --> 00:01:06.380
and the gap between the curves grows fast.

26
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.880
For one hundred products

27
00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:11.185
factoring takes three hundred

28
00:01:11.261 --> 00:01:13.412
twenty eight thousand steps

29
00:01:13.489 --> 00:01:15.793
and each order then takes nine

30
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.560
thousand nine hundred.

31
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.128
Eliminating from scratch for a thousand

32
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.428
orders costs about three hundred thirty

33
00:01:24.511 --> 00:01:26.161
eight million steps.

34
00:01:26.161 --> 00:01:29.462
Factoring once costs about ten million.

35
00:01:29.462 --> 00:01:32.184
That is thirty three times less

36
00:01:32.184 --> 00:01:34.412
and the bigger the matrix

37
00:01:34.412 --> 00:01:36.310
the bigger the saving.

38
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.470
And do not compute the inverse

39
00:01:39.564 --> 00:01:40.875
just to solve.

40
00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:45.091
Finding A inverse costs about n cubed steps

41
00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:47.620
three times the factoring.

42
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.065
Some matrices are much cheaper.

43
00:01:51.157 --> 00:01:53.560
A temperature sensor gives

44
00:01:53.652 --> 00:01:55.408
six noisy readings.

45
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.272
We want a smoothed series that

46
00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:00.952
stays close to the readings

47
00:02:00.952 --> 00:02:02.800
and changes slowly.

48
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.321
Minimising both errors gives

49
00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:07.337
one equation per reading

50
00:02:07.415 --> 00:02:09.819
and each smoothed value is tied

51
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:11.990
only to its two neighbours.

52
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.596
So the matrix is tridiagonal

53
00:02:14.673 --> 00:02:16.851
nonzero only on the diagonal

54
00:02:16.929 --> 00:02:18.562
and right next to it.

55
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.673
The smoothed values run from about four

56
00:02:21.751 --> 00:02:24.318
point eight to seven point seven

57
00:02:24.318 --> 00:02:26.340
instead of three to nine.

58
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.213
Now factor T.

59
00:02:28.312 --> 00:02:30.898
L has one entry below each

60
00:02:30.997 --> 00:02:32.986
one on its diagonal

61
00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:35.672
and U has one entry to the

62
00:02:35.771 --> 00:02:37.760
right of each pivot.

63
00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.820
The zeros stay zeros

64
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.507
so each step changes one number.

65
00:02:42.507 --> 00:02:44.624
For two hundred readings

66
00:02:44.705 --> 00:02:47.148
a simple loop visits two point

67
00:02:47.229 --> 00:02:48.858
six million entries

68
00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:51.545
but only one hundred ninety nine

69
00:02:51.626 --> 00:02:54.150
of those steps change anything.

70
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.327
The work grows like n, not n cubed.

71
00:02:57.327 --> 00:03:00.962
A million readings take a few million steps

72
00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:04.274
a full matrix that size would need about

73
00:03:04.354 --> 00:03:07.020
three times ten to the seventeen.

74
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:08.793
Now a warning.

75
00:03:08.793 --> 00:03:12.117
Here is the matrix B from lecture two.

76
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:13.991
After the first step

77
00:03:13.991 --> 00:03:17.400
the second pivot position holds a zero.

78
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.138
We cannot divide by zero

79
00:03:20.138 --> 00:03:22.813
so plain elimination stops.

80
00:03:22.813 --> 00:03:24.437
B is invertible

81
00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:27.590
but it has no L U factorization.

82
00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.393
So this belief is false

83
00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:33.449
that every invertible matrix can

84
00:03:33.542 --> 00:03:35.765
be written as L times U

85
00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:37.895
with no row exchanges.

86
00:03:37.895 --> 00:03:40.210
B is the counterexample.

87
00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.506
The fix is to do the exchanges first.

88
00:03:44.606 --> 00:03:47.719
A permutation matrix P puts row

89
00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:50.330
three of B above row two.

90
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.142
Now every pivot is nonzero

91
00:03:53.142 --> 00:03:55.150
and P B equals L U.

92
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.072
Every invertible matrix has a factorization

93
00:03:59.155 --> 00:04:00.402
P A equals L U.

94
00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:04.057
That is what numpy's solve computes inside.

95
00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.715
Real solvers also exchange rows

96
00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:09.208
when a pivot is merely small

97
00:04:09.208 --> 00:04:11.700
to keep rounding errors down.

98
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.520
Last, a habit for working with AI.

99
00:04:15.615 --> 00:04:18.283
Suppose an assistant factors

100
00:04:18.378 --> 00:04:19.998
the paint matrix.

101
00:04:19.998 --> 00:04:24.000
Its L is right, but its U is this matrix.

102
00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.919
Two checks, one line each.

103
00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:30.377
First, U must be upper triangular

104
00:04:30.377 --> 00:04:34.824
and this one has a three below the diagonal.

105
00:04:34.824 --> 00:04:37.986
Second, L times U must equal A

106
00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:40.950
and here the last row is off.

107
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.586
The assistant stopped one step early.

108
00:04:44.586 --> 00:04:46.540
Always multiply back.

109
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:48.026
To sum up.

110
00:04:48.026 --> 00:04:51.831
Factoring costs about n cubed over three

111
00:04:51.831 --> 00:04:55.817
each solve about n squared, so factor once.

112
00:04:55.817 --> 00:04:58.534
A banded matrix stays banded

113
00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:00.255
and costs about n.

114
00:05:00.255 --> 00:05:04.151
And for a zero pivot, exchange rows first

115
00:05:04.151 --> 00:05:05.600
P A equals L U.

116
00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.364
Now you try: factor the matrix three, one

117
00:05:10.364 --> 00:05:13.518
six, five, and multiply back.

118
00:05:13.518 --> 00:05:17.722
Next lecture: transposes, permutations

119
00:05:17.722 --> 00:05:21.190
and the first spaces of vectors.
